Approach: Largest Rectangle in Histogram
The main idea is to find, for every bar, how far it can extend to the left and right while maintaining its height.

For a bar i with height heights[i]:

PSE[i] = index of the first smaller element on the left.
NSE[i] = index of the first smaller element on the right.
Therefore, the bar can extend from PSE[i] + 1 to NSE[i] - 1.
So:

width = NSE[i] - PSE[i] - 1

and

area = width × heights[i]



Learn more
1. Find NSE — Next Smaller Element
Traverse right to left using a monotonic increasing stack.

For each index i:

Remove elements from the stack while their height is >= heights[i].
After removing:
If stack is empty → no smaller element exists → NSE[i] = n
Otherwise → NSE[i] = st.top()
Push i.
For example:

heights = [2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 3]

NSE = [1, 6, 4, 4, 6, 6]

2. Find PSE — Previous Smaller Element
Traverse left to right.

For each index i:

Remove elements whose height is >= heights[i].
After removing:
If stack is empty → PSE[i] = -1
Otherwise → PSE[i] = st.top()
Push i.
For the same example:

PSE = [-1, -1, 1, 2, 1, 4]

3. Calculate the maximum area
Now every bar knows its left and right boundaries.

For each i:

int width = NSE[i] - PSE[i] - 1;
int area = width * heights[i];

maxi = max(maxi, area);

For:

[2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 3]

Consider height 5 at index 2:

PSE[2] = 1
NSE[2] = 4

width = 4 - 1 - 1
      = 2

area = 2 * 5
     = 10

So the maximum rectangle is 10.

Why the stack works
The stack maintains indices whose heights are in increasing order.

When we encounter a smaller height, we pop taller bars because we have just discovered their boundary.

Each index is:

pushed once
popped at most once
Therefore, although there is a while loop, the total stack operations are linear.

Complexity
Your complexity comments can be simplified:

getNSE() → O(n)
getPSE() → O(n)
Final loop → O(n)
Total → O(n)
Extra space → O(n)
So the final complexity is:

Time Complexity:  O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)



Code-
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> getNSE( vector<int>& heights , int n ) {

        stack<int> st ;
        vector<int> NSE(n,0) ;

        for ( int i = n-1 ; i >= 0 ; i-- ) {

            while( !st.empty() && heights[st.top()] >=  heights[i] ) st.pop() ;

            if ( st.empty() ) NSE[i] = n ;

            else NSE[i] = st.top() ;

            st.push(i) ;

        }

        return NSE ;

    }

        vector<int> getPSE( vector<int>& heights , int n ) {

        stack<int> st ;
        vector<int> PSE(n,0) ;

        for ( int i = 0 ; i < n ; i++ ) {

            while( !st.empty() && heights[st.top()] >=  heights[i] ) st.pop() ;

            if ( st.empty() ) PSE[i] = -1 ;

            else PSE[i] = st.top() ;

            st.push(i) ;

        }

        return PSE ;

    }

    int largestRectangleArea(vector<int>& heights) {

        int n = heights.size() ;
        
        // NSE --> next smaller element
        // PSE --> previous smaller element
        vector<int> NSE = getNSE(heights,n) ;
        vector<int> PSE = getPSE(heights,n) ;

        int maxi = 0 ;

        for ( int i = 0 ; i < n ; i++ ) {

            // area of rectangle = L X B or width * height
            int length = NSE[i] - PSE[i] - 1 ;
            int area = length * heights[i] ;
            
            maxi = max( maxi , area ) ;

        }

        return maxi ;

        // getNSE()
        // for loop → O(n)
        // while → O(n) amortized (each element pushed/popped once)
        // Vector + stack space → O(2n)
        // T.C = O(2n), S.C = O(2n)

        // getPSE()
        // Same logic
        // T.C = O(2n), S.C = O(2n)

        // largestRectangleArea()
        // getNSE() → O(2n)
        // getPSE() → O(2n)
        // Final for loop → O(n)

        // So:

        // T.C = O(2n) + O(2n) + O(2n) = O(5n)
        // S.C = O(n)

        // Final: Time = O(5n), Space = O(3n).
        
    }
};
